  #block(breakable: false)[
  == Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Wants are unlimited desires, whether or not a person can pay for them. What a person can afford is effective demand, and the needs of survival are only a small part of wants.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    It is the ranking of wants that makes choice possible. The price-and-quantity table is a demand schedule, and the income record is a budget.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone, which here is the hospital. The money spent is the money cost — a different and much smaller idea.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The economy can now produce more of both goods, which is growth. A movement inside the curve is unemployment, and a movement along it is reallocation.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The frontier has not moved; the economy has merely stopped wasting capacity. New technology would push the whole curve outward.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A straight line means each extra unit of one good costs the same amount of the other, whatever the mix. The bowed-out curve is the realistic case of increasing cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Money buys factors but is not one: it produces nothing by itself. The building is capital, the labourer is labour and the entrepreneur organises the other three.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Labour, including both manual and mental work. Land earns rent, capital earns interest and the entrepreneur earns profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Land means every free gift of nature. Restricting it to farmland is the everyday meaning, not the economic one.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Prices carry the information and the incentive. A planning committee is the answer in a command economy.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Private enterprise and public enterprise coexist, which describes almost every real economy, Nigeria included.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The plan replaces the price as the coordinating device. Profit, free entry and consumer sovereignty are features of a market economy.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Distribution — who gets how much of the product. What to produce concerns the mix, and how to produce concerns the technique.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The value of the one alternative that would otherwise have been chosen. Summing every alternative gives a figure no decision-maker ever gives up.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Air is the standard example: no scarcity, so no sacrifice to obtain another unit. A subsidised good is still scarce and still has an opportunity cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Inside the curve means some resources are idle or badly used. Every point on the curve is efficient, and every point outside it is unattainable.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Better technology raises what the same resources can produce. Tastes and prices change what is demanded, not what is possible, and unemployment moves the economy inside the curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    If resources were unlimited, no choice would be necessary and nothing would be given up. Every other problem on the list is a consequence, not the source.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A consumer good satisfies a want directly. A capital good is used to make other goods, and an intermediate good is used up in producing them.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Production creates utility, and utility is created when the good reaches the person who uses it. Transport, storage and retailing are therefore part of production, not after it.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The law of demand. Its mirror image, a direct relationship, is the law of supply, and diminishing returns is a statement about production.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    At each price the individual quantities are added, which is a horizontal sum. Adding prices instead would sum the curves vertically and answer a different question.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The substitution effect and the income effect work together. The third option describes the supply curve, and the fourth invents a rule that does not exist.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Desire alone is not demand; it must be backed by purchasing power. A want without money is a wish, not an economic demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    More income means more bought at every price, so the curve shifts right. For an inferior good the same rise shifts it left.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Cheaper printers mean more printers sold and therefore more ink cartridges demanded. Complements are used together, so their demands move together.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Demand falling as income rises is the definition of an inferior good, with a negative income elasticity. A Giffen good is a special inferior good whose demand also rises with its own price.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Conspicuous consumption — the Veblen effect. A Giffen good also breaks the law, but through the income effect on a staple, not through display.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    More buyers mean more bought at every price. Population is a determinant of demand, and a larger population shifts the whole curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Buyers stock up before the increase, so current demand shifts right. Expectation of a future price change acts on demand today.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The sum of the satisfaction from every unit. The satisfaction from the last unit is marginal utility, and the satisfaction per naira is the ratio the consumer equalises.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A negative marginal utility means the last unit made the consumer worse off, so total utility has turned down. At the maximum of total utility, marginal utility is exactly zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Each extra unit adds less than the one before. Total utility is still rising while marginal utility is positive; it falls only when marginal utility turns negative.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Utility per naira must be equal across goods. Equating the marginal utilities would be right only if the prices were also equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The last naira spent on X is buying more satisfaction than the last naira spent on Y, so spending should move towards X until the two ratios are equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Cardinal utility assumes satisfaction can be measured in numbers. The ordinal approach ranks bundles instead and uses indifference curves.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Every point on one indifference curve yields equal satisfaction, which is why the consumer does not mind which point is chosen. The highest attainable curve is a different question.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The affordable set. The point chosen on it is where the budget line touches the highest indifference curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    More income moves both intercepts out by the same proportion, so the line shifts parallel. A change in one price rotates the line instead.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A lower price moves the buyer down and along the curve to a larger quantity — an extension of demand. A rise in price would cause a contraction; neither shifts the curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(41, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The law of supply, and it is why the supply curve slopes upward. The law of variable proportions is the short-run production law.
  ]

  #sol-item(42, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A table of price and quantity supplied. The graph of the same data is the supply curve, and the stock in the warehouse is inventory, not supply.
  ]

  #sol-item(43, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Beyond a point, extra output costs more per unit, so a higher price is needed to make it worth producing. The first option confuses supply with demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(44, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Better technology lowers cost, so more is supplied at every price. The good's own price moves the seller along the curve, and income and complements act on demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(45, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The tax is an extra cost at every quantity, so less is offered at every price. A subsidy would shift the curve the other way.
  ]

  #sol-item(46, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Producers can offer less at every price, so supply shifts left and the price rises. The demand curve has not moved; the change is on the supply side.
  ]

  #sol-item(47, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The price that clears the market, with neither a surplus nor a shortage. Any other price sets up a force that pushes the market back towards it.
  ]

  #sol-item(48, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Buyers want more than sellers offer, so unsatisfied buyers bid the price up. A surplus is the result of a price above equilibrium.
  ]

  #sol-item(49, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Unmet demand is a queue, and a queue bids the price up until the excess disappears. Excess supply does the opposite.
  ]

  #sol-item(50, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A ceiling above equilibrium is not binding, because the market price is already lower. Only a ceiling below equilibrium forces a shortage.
  ]

  #sol-item(51, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A floor above equilibrium is usually a producer-support measure, and minimum wage legislation is its labour-market version. A ceiling protects buyers and is set below equilibrium.
  ]

  #sol-item(52, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The demand curve shifts right along an upward-sloping supply curve, so both the equilibrium price and quantity rise. That combination is the signature of a demand increase.
  ]

  #sol-item(53, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Both shifts push quantity down, so quantity must fall. They push price in opposite directions, so the price change is indeterminate.
  ]

  #sol-item(54, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The tax shifts supply left, so the price rises and quantity falls. Part of the tax is passed to buyers, which is why the price does not rise by the full amount of the tax.
  ]

  #sol-item(55, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The side that cannot escape bears the tax. Inelastic buyers cannot walk away, so they pay; elastic producers can, so they do not.
  ]

  #sol-item(56, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The subsidy raises the buyers' effective purchasing power, so demand shifts right. A subsidy to producers would shift supply right instead.
  ]

  #sol-item(57, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Prices ration scarce goods, signal where demand is strong, and reward the producers who respond. Taxation, borrowing and spending are government instruments.
  ]

  #sol-item(58, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Stability means self-correction: a surplus pushes the price down, a shortage pushes it up, and both roads lead back to the same point.
  ]

  #sol-item(59, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Both shifts raise quantity, but only demand raises price; because the demand shift is larger, the price rises too. Equal shifts would leave the price unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(60, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    It is the gain from selling above the lowest acceptable price, and it is the area above the supply curve and below the price. The gap between price and average cost measures profit, not surplus.
  ]

  #sol-item(61, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Responsiveness, and it is measured in percentages so that units cancel. Slope is a different idea: a steep curve may still be elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(62, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Percentage changes, so that the answer is unit-free. Using absolute changes gives a figure that depends on the units chosen.
  ]

  #sol-item(63, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Zero: quantity does not change at all, and the demand curve is vertical. Infinite elasticity is the other extreme, a horizontal curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(64, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Any rise in price loses every buyer, so quantity collapses to zero — a horizontal line at the ruling price. Perfect competition's individual firm faces this curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(65, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Above one, quantity responds proportionately more than price. Between zero and one is inelastic, and exactly one is unit elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(66, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Quantity falls proportionately more than price rises, so the quantity effect dominates and revenue falls. This is the revenue test in one sentence.
  ]

  #sol-item(67, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The two effects are exactly equal and cancel, so total revenue stays put. Revenue is at its maximum at this point on a straight-line demand curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(68, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Substitutes give the buyer somewhere else to go. Necessity, a small budget share and a short time period all push towards inelasticity.
  ]

  #sol-item(69, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    No substitute and no postponement, so buyers pay whatever is asked. Price is not the same as elasticity of demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(70, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A large budget share makes buyers sensitive to the price. The same proportion of income test explains why salt is inelastic and a car is elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(71, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Buyers find substitutes and change habits given time, so responsiveness rises. Demand is always more elastic in the long run than in the short run.
  ]

  #sol-item(72, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    PES = 20 ÷ 10 = 2.0, which is above one, so supply is elastic. Inverting the division gives 0.5, the usual slip.
  ]

  #sol-item(73, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Capacity cannot be expanded at once, so output responds only slowly. In the long run all factors can change and supply becomes more elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(74, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    PED = 8 ÷ 12 = 0.67, which is below one, so demand is inelastic. The 1.5 figures invert the division.
  ]

  #sol-item(75, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Quantity falls 20 per cent and price rises 20 per cent, so PED = 1.0. Comparing absolute changes instead gives 10 ÷ 2 = 5, the commonest arithmetic error in the topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(76, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Positive but below one: demand rises with income, but proportionately less, which is the definition of a necessity. A luxury has an elasticity above one.
  ]

  #sol-item(77, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Above one, so demand rises faster than income — a luxury. Necessities sit between zero and one, and inferior goods are negative.
  ]

  #sol-item(78, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Zero responsiveness to income, which is the mark of a good consumed in a fixed amount whatever the household earns. Inferior goods are negative, not zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(79, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    No relationship: a change in the price of one leaves the demand for the other untouched. Positive means substitutes and negative means complements.
  ]

  #sol-item(80, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The inelastic side absorbs the tax. Producers cannot cut output much, so they accept a lower net price while the market price barely moves.
  ]

  #sol-item(81, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Inputs to maximum output, given the technology. It is a physical relationship; cost and revenue enter only after the output is known.
  ]

  #sol-item(82, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    One fixed factor defines the short run, and it is what makes the law of diminishing returns possible. In the long run every factor is variable.
  ]

  #sol-item(83, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Each extra worker adds less than the one before, so total product still rises but more slowly. Economies of scale is a long-run statement about average cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(84, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Output per worker. The change in output over the change in workers is marginal product, which is a different and more sensitive measure.
  ]

  #sol-item(85, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The change in the total. Marginal values are always changes in totals, never totals or averages themselves.
  ]

  #sol-item(86, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    While marginal product is above average product, the average is rising; when it falls below, the average falls. They meet at the peak of the average.
  ]

  #sol-item(87, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Stage two: output still rises with more labour, but less than proportionately. Stage three, with negative marginal product, is always irrational.
  ]

  #sol-item(88, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The law bites when each extra worker adds less than the one before, which happens while total product is still rising. Total product falls much later.
  ]

  #sol-item(89, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Rent is payable whatever the output, so it does not vary with production. The other three rise and fall with the level of output.
  ]

  #sol-item(90, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Packaging is used up in proportion to output. Insurance, a director's salary and rent are fixed in the short run.
  ]

  #sol-item(91, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    TC = TFC + TVC. Adding fixed cost to marginal cost mixes a total with a per-unit figure and gives a meaningless number.
  ]

  #sol-item(92, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Cost per unit. Dividing only the fixed cost gives average fixed cost, and the change in total cost gives marginal cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(93, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The extra cost of the extra unit. Fixed cost does not change with output, so the marginal cost is entirely a variable-cost idea.
  ]

  #sol-item(94, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Below the minimum, marginal cost is the cheaper unit that pulls the average down; above it, the dearer unit pulls the average up. That is why the crossing is always at the minimum.
  ]

  #sol-item(95, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A constant total spread over a growing output must fall, and it never reaches zero. The U shape belongs to average total cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(96, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Every factor can be changed, so no cost is fixed. That is why the long-run average cost curve is built from the lowest point of each short-run curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(97, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Internal economies come from within — bulk buying, specialisation, better finance. Growth of the whole industry produces external economies, which benefit every firm.
  ]

  #sol-item(98, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Rising long-run average cost as the firm grows is diseconomies of scale. Diminishing returns is a short-run law and would be drawn on a different set of curves.
  ]

  #sol-item(99, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Zero economic profit: the firm covers every cost, including normal profit, and no more. Maximum profit is a different point, where MR = MC.
  ]

  #sol-item(100, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Revenue per unit above cost per unit leaves a surplus after every cost is paid. AR = AC is normal profit, which is the break-even condition.
  ]

  #sol-item(101, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The organisational features that determine how firms behave: numbers, product type, entry and information. The physical market place has nothing to do with it.
  ]

  #sol-item(102, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The standard spectrum from many sellers to one. A cartel is a behaviour found inside oligopoly, not a structure in its own right.
  ]

  #sol-item(103, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Many sellers, a homogeneous product, free entry and perfect information. Differentiation and a few large firms belong to monopolistic competition and oligopoly.
  ]

  #sol-item(104, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The firm is a price taker, so every extra unit sells at the same price and MR = P = AR. A price maker would have MR below price.
  ]

  #sol-item(105, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Since P = MR for a price taker, MR = MC becomes P = MC. The other conditions either describe break-even or maximise revenue rather than profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(106, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Below minimum AVC the firm cannot even cover the cost of the inputs it could switch off, so every unit produced adds to the loss. Between AVC and AC it loses money but loses less by producing.
  ]

  #sol-item(107, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Many sellers and free entry are competitive; differentiation is what separates it from perfect competition and gives each firm a little price power.
  ]

  #sol-item(108, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Free entry drives the demand curve down to the point of tangency with average cost, so profit is normal and output is below minimum-cost scale. That gap is excess capacity.
  ]

  #sol-item(109, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A patent, a licence or control of a resource keeps rivals out, and that protection is what allows the monopoly to persist. Many firms, a homogeneous product and perfect information are competitive features.
  ]

  #sol-item(110, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A price cut applies to all units, not only the extra one, so the gain from the extra unit is partly offset. Under perfect competition the price is constant, so MR equals price.
  ]

  #sol-item(111, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Declining long-run average cost means one firm can supply the market more cheaply than two, as with water and electricity networks. Legal protection is a statutory monopoly, which is a different source of the same power.
  ]

  #sol-item(112, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Output is set by MR = MC, and the price comes from the demand curve at that output. Charging marginal cost would be the competitive rule and would give away the profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(113, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Interdependence is the defining feature: each firm must guess what its rivals will do. That is why oligopoly models rely on assumptions about rival behaviour.
  ]

  #sol-item(114, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Rivals are assumed to match a price cut but not a price rise, so demand is elastic above the ruling price and inelastic below it, and marginal revenue has a vertical gap. The firm has no incentive to move.
  ]

  #sol-item(115, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Members behave like a single monopolist, so price rises and output is held down. The high profit is exactly what tempts members to cheat, which is why cartels are unstable.
  ]

  #sol-item(116, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A recognised leader — often the largest or lowest-cost firm — announces a price and the rest match it, which achieves coordination without a formal agreement.
  ]

  #sol-item(117, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Every buyer pays their own reservation price, so the whole consumer surplus is extracted. Second-degree discrimination charges different prices for different quantities, and third-degree separates market segments.
  ]

  #sol-item(118, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The price buyers pay equals the cost of the last unit produced, so exactly the right quantity is made. Price equal to average cost is productive efficiency and normal profit, not allocative efficiency.
  ]

  #sol-item(119, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Restricting output is how the monopolist keeps the price up, which is exactly why the market is allocatively inefficient. Charging a higher price and producing more would require demand to cooperate, and it does not.
  ]

  #sol-item(120, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Contestability depends on the absence of sunk costs rather than on the number of firms. If a rival could enter cheaply, the incumbent must keep prices near cost to avoid inviting it.
  ]

